# AAMAS 2027 - OpenReview "Official Review" form.
# Fields, wording and scales are copied from the OpenReview template.
# `help` = long beginner-oriented explanation shown in the app (not part of the submission).
# `private` fields are your own workbench: saved with the review, never exported.

Sitemap label="AAMAS 2027 - Official Review" venue="AAMAS 2027" kind=conference year=2027 fidelity=form
  source="https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/dcs/aamas2027/guidelines-and-policies/reviewer-guidelines/"
  description="Official Review form: summary, technical quality, significance, presentation, review, proceedings and findings ratings (1-10), confidence, best paper." {

  Frame label="Paper (private bookkeeping)" private {
    Text id=paper_title label="Paper title" private
    Text id=paper_id label="Submission number" private placeholder="e.g. 1234"
  }

  Info label="How to use this form" text="""
    The form below mirrors the OpenReview form field by field, in the same order.
    Click "What does this mean?" under any field for a plain-language explanation,
    what a good answer looks like, and common beginner mistakes.

    Suggested workflow: (1) read the paper once quickly, (2) fill the private workbench
    at the bottom while reading carefully, (3) write Summary, then Review, (4) only then
    pick the scores, so that scores follow from your arguments, not the other way round.

    Reminder from the template: you may not upload the paper into a generative AI tool such as ChatGPT.
  """

  Frame label="Written part" {

    Markdown id=summary label="Summary" required hint="Briefly summarise the main contribution of the paper." help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      A short, neutral description of what the paper does. It is NOT where you judge the paper.
      The template also reminds you that you may not upload the paper to a generative AI tool.

      WHY IT EXISTS
      (1) It proves you understood the paper. (2) The authors and the Area Chair can check whether
      their message came across: if your summary is wrong, the paper is unclear. (3) It lets the Area Chair
      (who may not have time to read the paper) see what the paper claims.

      WHAT A GOOD SUMMARY CONTAINS (about 5-10 lines)
      - The problem: what is the paper trying to solve, and why is that hard?
      - The idea: what do the authors propose (method, theory, benchmark, analysis...)?
      - The evidence: how is it supported (theorems, experiments, which benchmarks, which baselines)?
      - The headline result: what do the authors claim they achieve?

      TIPS
      - Write it in your own words, in a neutral tone: describe claims as "the authors claim / show / propose".
      - Do not put criticism or praise here. Save it for the Review field.
      - Test: could the authors read it and say "yes, that is what we did"? If so, it is good.

      COMMON MISTAKES
      - Copying the abstract: it shows nothing about your understanding.
      - Describing only the problem and not the contribution.
      - Slipping judgements in ("a nice but incremental idea").
    """

    Frame label="Scores" {

      Choice id=technical label="Technical Quality" required hint="Please assess the technical quality of the paper." help="""
        WHAT IS ASKED
        Is the work correct and well supported? This is about SOUNDNESS, not about whether the topic is exciting.

        WHAT TO CHECK
        - Are the claims actually backed by the theory or the experiments shown?
        - Theory: are the assumptions stated and reasonable? Do the proofs look right (check at least the key steps)?
        - Experiments: are the baselines strong and fairly tuned? Enough seeds / runs and reported variance? Are the
          benchmarks appropriate? Do ablations show which component matters? Are metrics well defined?
        - Do conclusions overreach the evidence (e.g. "robust" claimed from three environments)?
        - Is the method described precisely enough to be reproduced (hyperparameters, code, appendix)?

        HOW TO CHOOSE
        Anchor on the weakest important link: a beautiful idea with a flawed evaluation is not "Excellent".
        Typical flaws that lower the score: missing key baselines, unfair comparisons, unsupported claims,
        proofs with gaps, results within noise. If you find a flaw, point to it concretely in the Review field.

        Remember that this score is separate from Significance and Presentation: a paper can be sound but
        unimportant, or important but sloppily written. Score each dimension independently.
      """ {
        Option value=5 label="5. Excellent" help="Rigorous and convincing. Claims are fully supported; evaluation is thorough and fair. You could not find meaningful technical weaknesses."
        Option value=4 label="4. Very Good" help="Solid. Minor weaknesses (a missing ablation, a small gap) that do not threaten the main claims."
        Option value=3 label="3. Acceptable" help="Mostly sound, but with noticeable weaknesses or some claims only partly supported. Fixable in a revision."
        Option value=2 label="2. Flawed" help="Significant problems: key claims are not supported, evaluation is inadequate or unfair, or there are errors that matter."
        Option value=1 label="1. Fatally Flawed" help="The central claims are wrong or cannot be trusted (e.g. a proof is incorrect, the experiments do not test the claim). Use rarely, and justify precisely."
      }

      Choice id=significance label="Significance And Relevance" required hint="Please assess the significance and relevance of the paper." help="""
        WHAT IS ASKED
        Two questions in one: (a) Does the paper belong at AAMAS (agents and multiagent systems)?
        (b) If it works, would it matter - to how many people in the community?

        WHAT TO CONSIDER
        - Relevance: agents, multiagent learning, coordination, game theory, social choice, agent-based modelling,
          human-agent interaction, etc. A pure single-agent paper with a token "multi-agent" mention is borderline.
        - Impact: does it open a new direction, solve a known open problem, or give a tool others will use?
          Or is it a small tweak on an existing method?
        - Novelty matters here: how different is it from prior work? Check related work you know, and the paper's own
          positioning. Say which prior works it is close to.
        - Scope: a result limited to one narrow setting is significant for a subfield at most.

        HOW TO CHOOSE
        The scale is about BREADTH of audience: whole AAMAS area (5) down to a subfield (3-2) to out of scope (1).
        Do not downgrade just because the topic is not YOUR topic, and do not upgrade just because it is
        fashionable. If you are unsure about novelty, say so in the Review and lower your Confidence.
      """ {
        Option value=5 label="5. Potentially highly impactful for the entire AAMAS area" help="Could change how many AAMAS researchers think or work. Rare."
        Option value=4 label="4. Significant for large parts of the AAMAS community" help="Of clear interest to several AAMAS subareas."
        Option value=3 label="3. Significant for an AAMAS subfield" help="Solid contribution that matters to people working on this specific topic."
        Option value=2 label="2. Some significance for an AAMAS subfield" help="Incremental or narrow; modest value even for the subfield."
        Option value=1 label="1. Not relevant to AAMAS" help="Out of scope for the conference."
      }

      Choice id=presentation label="Presentation Quality" required hint="Please assess the presentation quality of the paper." help="""
        WHAT IS ASKED
        How clearly is the paper written and organised? This is about communication, not about the science itself.

        WHAT TO CHECK
        - Could an informed AAMAS reader follow the paper and reproduce the idea from the text alone?
        - Are the problem, notation and method defined before use? Is notation consistent?
        - Do figures and tables help and have readable captions/axes? Are results easy to find?
        - Is the structure logical (motivation -> method -> experiments -> discussion)? Are limitations stated?
        - Is related work covered fairly? Is the language understandable?
        - Does it respect the formatting and page limits?

        HOW TO CHOOSE
        Minor typos do not matter much; confusing notation, undefined terms, or unreadable plots do. If you needed
        to guess what the authors meant, that is a presentation problem: give concrete examples in the Review so the
        authors can fix it. Do not let writing quality leak into the Technical Quality score, and vice versa.
      """ {
        Option value=5 label="5. Excellent" help="Clear, well organised, a pleasure to read."
        Option value=4 label="4. Very Good" help="Clear overall; minor issues only."
        Option value=3 label="3. Acceptable" help="Understandable but with parts needing effort or rewriting."
        Option value=2 label="2. Poor" help="Hard to follow; important parts unclear, which impedes judging the work."
        Option value=1 label="1. Unacceptable" help="Cannot be understood or evaluated in its current form."
      }
    }

    Markdown id=review label="Review" required hint="Evaluation of quality, clarity, originality and significance, including a list of pros and cons (max 200000 characters). Markdown and LaTeX are supported." help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      The main body of your review: a reasoned evaluation of the paper's quality, clarity, originality and significance,
      with an explicit list of pros and cons. Markdown is supported, and so are LaTeX formulas ($...$).
      The OpenReview limit is 200000 characters: you will never need that much.

      WHAT THE READERS DO WITH IT
      The authors use it to improve the paper or to rebut. The Area Chair uses it (with the other reviews) to decide.
      So it has to justify your scores to someone who has not read the paper closely.

      A STRUCTURE THAT WORKS
      1. Overall assessment (2-4 sentences): your verdict and the main reasons.
      2. Strengths (pros): bullet points, specific ("the ablation in Table 3 isolates X")
         rather than generic ("well written").
      3. Weaknesses (cons): bullet points ordered by importance. For each: what is the issue, where in the paper,
         why it matters, and what would fix it.
      4. Detailed comments by dimension:
         - Quality (technical soundness): claims vs. evidence, assumptions, proofs, experimental design.
         - Clarity: what was hard to follow, notation, figures.
         - Originality: relation to prior work; name the closest papers if you know them.
         - Significance: who would care and why.
      5. Questions for the authors: things whose answers could change your mind. Number them so the
         authors can reply one by one.
      6. Minor comments: typos, small presentation details (clearly separated from the major points).
      7. Optional: what would raise your score (concrete changes or experiments).

      TIPS FOR A NEW REVIEWER
      - Be specific and constructive; criticise the work, never the authors. Write what you would want to receive.
      - Back every criticism with a reason or a reference to a section, equation, figure or table.
      - Separate facts ("Table 2 has no variance") from opinions ("I find the benchmark weak").
      - Do not penalise a paper for not doing something outside its stated scope, but do flag overclaims.
      - Do not ask for endless extra experiments: request those that test the central claim.
      - Say what you did NOT check (e.g. "I did not verify the proofs in the appendix").
      - Disclose uncertainty; it is better than faking expertise (see Confidence).
      - Check the reviewer guidelines linked in the template for the venue's rules on conduct and confidentiality.
      - The scores you picked must be consistent with this text. If you write mostly weaknesses, a high score will
        confuse the Area Chair.
    """
  }

  Frame label="Recommendation" {

    Choice id=rating_proceedings label="Rating Proceedings" required hint="Overall recommendation for acceptance in the Proceedings of AAMAS 2027." help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      Your overall verdict: should this paper be accepted to the main Proceedings of AAMAS 2027?
      This is the number the Area Chair looks at first, and it should summarise ALL your assessments: technical
      quality, significance, presentation and originality.

      HOW TO CHOOSE
      Ask: "Would I be comfortable seeing this paper in the proceedings as it is, with at most minor edits?"
      - Yes, happily -> 7 or more. Yes, but only just -> 6.
      - Not as it is, but it is close and fixable -> 5.
      - Real problems remain -> 4 or lower.
      Weigh the severity of problems, not their number. Technical soundness and significance usually weigh more than
      presentation, because presentation can be fixed in a revision and soundness cannot.

      CALIBRATION
      Scores are relative to the whole pool of AAMAS papers: the scale is defined by "top X% of accepted papers".
      Most papers sit in the middle (4-7). 9-10 are exceptional and need strong justification; 1-2 are reserved for
      trivial or seriously wrong work. Avoid always giving the same score by habit. The extremes carry heavy
      weight in the discussion, so make sure your text defends them.

      The decision between 5 and 6 is the threshold between rejecting and accepting: write in the Review what
      one specific change would flip it.
    """ {
      Option value=10 label="10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper" help="Seminal work. Extremely rare."
      Option value=9 label="9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept" help="Excellent in all respects; clearly stands out among accepted papers."
      Option value=8 label="8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept" help="Strong paper; you will argue for acceptance."
      Option value=7 label="7: Good paper, accept" help="Good, sound, worth publishing; weaknesses are minor."
      Option value=6 label="6: Marginally above acceptance threshold" help="Borderline but leaning accept: strengths slightly outweigh weaknesses."
      Option value=5 label="5: Marginally below acceptance threshold" help="Borderline but leaning reject: weaknesses slightly outweigh strengths, or fixable major issues."
      Option value=4 label="4: Ok but not good enough - rejection" help="Reasonable work, but not at the level required: significant weaknesses."
      Option value=3 label="3: Clear rejection" help="Serious problems in soundness, novelty or clarity."
      Option value=2 label="2: Strong rejection" help="Fundamentally weak; very unlikely to be fixable by revision."
      Option value=1 label="1: Trivial or wrong" help="The work is trivial or incorrect."
    }

    Choice id=rating_findings label="Rating Findings" required hint="If the paper is not selected for the Proceedings, your recommendation for the Findings of AAMAS 2027." help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      Papers not selected for the Proceedings are automatically considered for the "Findings of AAMAS 2027"
      (a secondary publication track), unless the authors opt out. This second score asks: if the paper does not
      make the main Proceedings, should it appear in Findings instead?

      The scale is the same as Rating Proceedings, but the bar is lower: Findings is for sound work that is useful
      to the community yet does not reach the Proceedings' threshold (for example solid but incremental, narrower
      in scope, or with limited novelty).

      HOW TO CHOOSE
      - Is the work technically sound and honest in its claims? If yes, a Findings recommendation is usually
        higher than or equal to your Proceedings score.
      - If the work is fundamentally flawed (wrong claims, unsound experiments), both scores should be low:
        Findings is not a place for papers that cannot be trusted.
      - It should never be lower than your Proceedings score for any sensible reason: a paper good enough for the
        Proceedings is also good enough for Findings.
      The submission page may show whether the authors opted out of Findings. If they did, check the venue's
      reviewer guidelines for how this score will be used.
    """ {
      Option value=10 label="10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper"
      Option value=9 label="9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept"
      Option value=8 label="8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept"
      Option value=7 label="7: Good paper, accept"
      Option value=6 label="6: Marginally above acceptance threshold"
      Option value=5 label="5: Marginally below acceptance threshold"
      Option value=4 label="4: Ok but not good enough - rejection"
      Option value=3 label="3: Clear rejection"
      Option value=2 label="2: Strong rejection"
      Option value=1 label="1: Trivial or wrong"
    }

    Choice id=confidence label="Confidence" required hint="Please indicate your confidence in this review." help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      How much should the Area Chair trust YOUR review? It is a statement about you, not about the paper.

      WHY IT MATTERS
      The Area Chair weights reviews by confidence. Overstating it makes a mistaken review count too much;
      understating it wastes a good one. Being honest here is a sign of a good reviewer, and being a newcomer
      is a legitimate reason for a lower number.

      HOW TO CHOOSE
      Ask yourself:
      - Do I know this subarea and its main prior works well enough to judge novelty? (relevance of literature)
      - Did I read the whole paper, including the appendix, and check the technical parts (proofs, experimental setup)?
      - Did I understand the central contribution?
      If you could not verify the proofs, or you are not sure about prior work, choose 3 or lower and say
      precisely what you did not check in the Review.
      Typical values: 5 is rare (expert in exactly this topic); 4 for an expert who skipped some details; 3 for
      a related field or a quick check; 2-1 when you are outside your area or struggled to understand.
    """ {
      Option value=5 label="5: The reviewer is absolutely certain that the evaluation is correct and very familiar with the relevant literature"
      Option value=4 label="4: The reviewer is confident but not absolutely certain that the evaluation is correct"
      Option value=3 label="3: The reviewer is fairly confident that the evaluation is correct"
      Option value=2 label="2: The reviewer is willing to defend the evaluation, but it is quite likely that the reviewer did not understand central parts of the paper"
      Option value=1 label="1: The reviewer's evaluation is an educated guess"
    }

    Checkbox id=best_paper label="I nominate this paper for a Best Paper Prize." hint="Optional: would you nominate this paper for a Best Paper Prize?" help="""
      WHAT IS ASKED
      An optional flag. Tick it only if you think this is among the very best papers at the conference.

      WHEN TO TICK
      Generally only when your Rating Proceedings is very high (typically 9-10) AND you can say why it stands out:
      a new idea, a convincing and unusually thorough evaluation, a clear writing. Say it in the Review.
      If you are not sure, leave it unticked: it is the exception, not the default.
    """
  }

  Frame label="Reviewer workbench (private - never exported)" private {

    Info label="About this section" text="Your own notes and checklist. They are saved with the review but excluded from the exported text."

    Checkbox id=chk_read label="First quick read done (abstract, intro, figures, conclusions)" private
    Checkbox id=chk_claims label="Listed the paper's claims and checked that each one is supported" private
    Checkbox id=chk_theory label="Checked assumptions / key proof steps" private
    Checkbox id=chk_exp label="Checked experimental design: baselines, seeds, variance, ablations, metrics" private
    Checkbox id=chk_related label="Checked related work and novelty claims" private
    Checkbox id=chk_repro label="Checked reproducibility (code, hyperparameters, appendix)" private
    Checkbox id=chk_ethics label="Checked for ethical / conflict-of-interest / dual-submission concerns" private
    Checkbox id=chk_consistent label="Scores are consistent with the text of my Review" private

    Textarea id=notes_questions label="Questions to ask the authors" private
    Textarea id=notes_page label="Notes while reading (page, issue)" private rows=10
  }
}
